WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:05.600
This video will show you how to get started
with Custom Translator, helping you to build

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:10.490
your custom neural machine translation models,
using your own data to fit your style and

00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:11.849
terminology.

00:00:11.849 --> 00:00:17.720
Once deployed, your custom system can be used
for both text and speech translation services.

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:21.670
There are four steps to build and use these
custom systems:

00:00:21.670 --> 00:00:23.260
1. Upload

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:25.000
2. Train

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:27.140
3. Deploy

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:29.220
4. Use in production

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:31.740
Let’s get started.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:39.280
First, go to customtranslator.ai, and log
into the custom translator portal.

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:43.500
Next, select the training documents for your
new model.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:49.380
Here’s an example of training documents:
English here and Japanese here.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:53.630
In this example, the sentences are not mapped.

00:00:53.630 --> 00:00:58.200
You can provide parallel data that is already
aligned, but if you don’t have that, for

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:03.860
example, if your content comes from a brochure
or a website, Custom Translator automatically

00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:06.970
matches sentences and aligns them for you.

00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:10.560
Next, select “New Text Project”.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:19.170
Input a Project name, Language pair for the
system you’re building, Category, and Project

00:01:19.170 --> 00:01:20.170
label.

00:01:20.170 --> 00:01:24.950
The label is important if you plan to build
more than one system with unique category

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:27.479
IDs for each project.

00:01:27.479 --> 00:01:32.330
Next, select the documents you uploaded in
step one, or upload additional ones that you

00:01:32.330 --> 00:01:36.510
want to include with this project’s training
dataset.

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:42.770
Select “Train”, then input a Model name,
and click “Train model”.

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:46.070
Training can take several hours depending
on the amount of data uploaded.

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:50.390
In your project view, navigate to the “Models”
tab.

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:54.049
If the training is complete, the “deploy”
button will be active.

00:01:54.049 --> 00:01:59.390
In the “BLEU” column, Custom Translator
automatically shows the result of its BLEU

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:00.830
score calculation.

00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:06.829
The BLEU score is an industry standard metric
to measure the difference between the automatic

00:02:06.829 --> 00:02:09.560
translation, and your reference translation.

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:14.510
This calculation is based on randomly selected
sentences extracted from the training set,

00:02:14.510 --> 00:02:16.520
and not used for actual training.

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:23.900
In this example, the green score - 38.83 - indicates
that the training of your custom model is

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:27.030
better than the original one.

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:31.230
Once you’re ready, click ‘deploy’, and
the system will be deployed to our production

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:33.550
servers within a few minutes.

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:36.700
Each training has a unique category ID.

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:42.790
To use this model, use this ID in the category
parameter of your calls to the Translator

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:43.790
API.

00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:48.220
Now that the system has been deployed, let’s
use it.

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:54.650
For this example, we’re running simple python
code in a free Azure Jupyter notebook.

00:02:54.650 --> 00:03:02.470
Input your Category ID, the text you want
to test, and the “To Language” you want

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:03.700
to translate to.

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:05.890
Here, we’ve chosen English.

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:11.440
For the output, we’re calling the translations
twice:

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:15.940
Once to translate content with no category
ID using the generic models,

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:22.650
and the second output to translate the content
using the custom model with Custom Translator.

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:26.239
Here we see the results:

00:03:26.239 --> 00:03:27.790
The text in English says:

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:32.630
“In earlier versions of Excel, only the
color of the lines that are included in the

00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:34.020
palette are supported.”

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:36.050
Not bad, but a bit wordy.

00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:41.099
The custom system built using Custom Translator
provides an English output of:

00:03:41.099 --> 00:03:45.790
“In earlier versions of Excel, only the
line color contained in the palette is supported.”

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:53.030
As you can see, it created a clearer, less
wordy translation.

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:57.230
Start building your own custom system and
visit customtranslator.ai

